hort is AGPL-3.0-only. The Apache Software Foundation publishes that Apache 2.0 software
may be included in a GPLv3 project; that statement names GPLv3 and does not name AGPLv3,
and this line says so rather than rounding it off.

Two things about the file itself, both measured rather than assumed, because either one
silently produces a profile that is not the one this project meant to carry.

It is NOT in OCI shape. It is Docker's profile format, which extends the OCI seccomp
object with a top-level archMap and defaultErrno and with per-entry includes, excludes and
comment. The Rust types this eventually becomes do not reject unknown fields, so a direct
deserialize succeeds and silently drops all of it. With an empty capability set, which is
what every hort container runs with, dropping the extensions turns eighteen
capability-gated groups into unconditional allow rules, twenty-four names in all, bpf and
chroot and open_by_handle_at among them. Resolving the extensions is therefore mandatory
rather than an optimisation. Note that setns is NOT one of them, however much it looks the
part: it sits in the unconditional allow group as well, so the naive route does not change
its status and what refuses it is the empty capability set. That mistake has already cost
this project one witness that stayed red against correct code.

A distribution's copy is not this copy. Fedora ships a comparable file at
/usr/share/containers/seccomp.json, and comparing the two is worth doing, but they differ:
as of containers-common 0.67.0 the distribution copy additionally refuses AF_VSOCK sockets
and restructures the netlink-audit rules, and that variant exists at no upstream revision
this file could be pinned to. What is carried here is the upstream, whole, at the revision
named above. The difference is small and known rather than accidental.
